#### Where the money goes

- Swap fees accrue inside pools. Protocol-fee collection and CAKE purchases are run by the Kitchen. CAKE ends up in the burn Safe 0xCEBA6028…4a0e, which calls CAKE transfer(0x…dEaD, amount) via execTransaction. Recent Safe burns (re-read on-chain from transaction receipts): 2026-11-02 07:47 UTC 58,498,043 CAKE (block 131,203,504, tx 0x5509023b842f0640351bdd69e5f4d45e0073a069b8543a2e66f33e04ee2d6c89); 2026-10-26 07:34 UTC 59,817,577 (block 129,858,472, tx 0x6a39b86fd807b38b50a1f969edf1e2d4f8d6e4cffd7004a2ba38310643679d66); 2026-10-19 06:10 UTC 58,928,655 (block 128,503,941, tx 0x88f2e899f6e48f2b8a5e6771db728caf4489c48be65c5916653bfb35caf24898). Each batch is mostly the 99.7% MasterChef emission discard plus the week's buyback CAKE.

- Exact buyback executor and fee-collector addresses are not published in the docs pages checked (Trading fees, CAKE Tokenomics; docs.pancakeswap.finance was serving pages normally on 2026-11-10).

- Treasury receives 9–19% of v3 fees and 9% of v2, all side-product fees (Prediction, Perps, etc.) since June 2026, and stablecoin-pool fees in stables since Feb 2026. None of this is distributed to holders.

#### Change underway: "The Flip" (passed 2026-10-24)

- v3 and Infinity on all 11 chains move to 88% LP / 12% protocol, and the 12% goes to Treasury. The proposal says "its non-stablecoin portion will still be used to buy back CAKE". It does not say the bought CAKE will be burned. v2 stays at 23% burn and becomes "our main CAKE burn engine".

- The rollout goes "in an order that protects CAKE burns": low-burn chains first, one per week for ~3 months, with emissions at 11,000/day. BNB Chain v3/Infinity switch last, when supply reaches the −20% target (~298.3M), "about 12 months after Step 1". Then emissions go to 7,000/day, all to the Pantry.

- Impact estimate (author's computation, not an official figure): over the last 4 weeks v3 + Infinity were 48.3% of buyback-burns (742,629 / 1,538,579 CAKE). Once BSC switches, burns at today's volumes would fall to ~199k CAKE/week (v2 + lottery), against 49k/week of emissions at 7,000/day. That is still net-deflationary, but it depends much more on v2 volume and on whether Treasury CAKE buybacks are burned.

