Older context still relevant: the sDOLA Llamalend oracle-manipulation exploit (2026-03-02) liquidated 27 borrowers for 822,475 crvUSD of losses; the attacker profit was about $245k net per the LlamaRisk post-mortem. Remediation is a veFunder gauge of up to 5M CRV [16][22]. The December 2025 rejection and amended passage of the Swiss Stake grant is also relevant [14].

### Current problems and risks

- Shrinking core business. TVL fell 46% year on year ($2.20B on 2025-11-10 → $1.18B on 2026-11-10; −50% from $2.36B on 2025-10-01), including a 25% drop from June to July 2026. DefiLlama fees ran $7.83M in Oct 2025 vs $4.47M in Oct 2026. On-chain veCRV payouts ran about $0.9–1.3M/month in Jul–Nov 2025 vs about $0.4–0.7M/month in H2 2026 (by week-start month) [3].

- Value capture is thin vs dilution. Holder payouts of about $6.8M/yr compare with about $24.6M/yr of emissions value and about CHF 5.3M/yr of Swiss Stake operating cost (grant-proposal figure, not re-verified) funded mostly in CRV. The plan to lift the treasury fee share to 30% would cut veCRV's cut by 22% (90 → 70) [7][14].

- Governance concentration. Convex holds 54% of veCRV and three lockers hold about 80%. Single-entity veto risk is real: Convex and Yearn sank the first 2026 Swiss Stake grant [14].

- Founder focus and conflicts. Egorov's engineering focus is on Yield Basis [17]. Curve's balance sheet supports YB through a 1B crvUSD credit ceiling, and YB pools pay Curve no admin fees on-chain. Critics in governance forums raised smart-contract and protocol-liability concerns, and risk-focused contributors warned rapid expansion could strain crvUSD peg mechanisms [20].

- Risk-management turnover. LlamaRisk left on 2026-06-30, and the new provider, yRisk, has been active since Sep 2026 [7][16].

- Security history. Incidents include the July 2023 Vyper-compiler reentrancy exploit on several pools (general knowledge, not re-sourced here), the June 2024 founder-loan bad debt of about $10M [17], the sDOLA Llamalend exploit (Mar 2026; caused by market oracle configuration, not core contracts) [22], and an April 2026 precautionary pause of LayerZero CRV bridging, resumed in June 2026 [16]. Each new Llamalend V2 collateral type (LP tokens, vault tokens) adds oracle risk.

- crvUSD scale. Mint-market debt is only about $52–57M, and supply is $143M (CoinGecko). That is small against GHO ($698M) or USDe ($4.95B) [3]. The borrow rate rose to 5.7% in October, which weighs on mint demand [8].

- Competition. Fluid DEX matches Curve's 30-day volume with about one fifth of its DEX TVL. Uniswap v4 hooks target stable pairs, and Aerodrome dominates Base [3].

- Market structure. CRV is down about 52% in 12 months ($0.524 on 2025-11-11 → $0.253 on 2026-11-10) and 98% from its ATH. Binance plus Hyperliquid perp OI is about $28.6M, roughly 7% of market cap, so derivatives positioning is a material short-term factor [2][23].

- What the market is hopeful about: Curve as neutral liquidity infrastructure for the expanding stablecoin universe (new issuers onboarding weekly, Arc, Stellar, FX corridors), Llamalend V2 turning idle LP and vault tokens into collateral (V2 borrowing up 45% in August [7]), and the fee spikes in volatile weeks, which showed fee capacity when markets move (veCRV APR 6.6% in the Binance-hack week) [6].

